1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
Tilt your head and look at
the back of your TV.

2
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:04,260
There's a row of ports.

3
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:10,700
One of them is an HDMI port, a small flat
plug with two clipped corners.

4
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:14,720
You've pushed a cable into it a dozen
times without a thought.

5
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:19,700
Before that TV left the factory, somebody
paid to put that plug there.

6
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:20,700
Not much.

7
00:00:20,980 --> 00:00:21,980
About four cents.

8
00:00:22,420 --> 00:00:23,980
You never saw it on the receipt.

9
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:26,790
It's folded into the price,
like salt in bread.

10
00:00:26,820 --> 00:00:31,500
On a four hundred dollar TV, it's one
dollar in every ten thousand.

11
00:00:31,740 --> 00:00:33,800
Now here's the part nobody mentions.

12
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:37,680
That plug isn't a standard that
belongs to everyone.

13
00:00:38,140 --> 00:00:42,980
It's a private contract, and it has been
collecting money for twenty-three years.

14
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:47,493
The money goes to a company called
HDMI Licensing Administrator,

15
00:00:47,513 --> 00:00:50,720
which collects for the companies
that built the plug.

16
00:00:51,180 --> 00:00:54,090
This video follows one chain
of cause and effect.

17
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:56,380
Each link forces the next.

18
00:00:56,720 --> 00:00:58,906
By the end, you'll know who gets paid,

19
00:00:58,926 --> 00:01:02,060
and why the four cents is the
smallest thing about it.

20
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:04,940
Start in 2002, with a mess.

21
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:08,480
Behind a TV of that year, you'd
find a knot of leads.

22
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:10,060
One for the picture.

23
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:11,360
Two for the sound.

24
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:13,860
Another if you wanted the picture sharper.

25
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:16,109
Every one was a different plug,

26
00:01:16,129 --> 00:01:19,280
made by a different company, and
they never quite fit.

27
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:22,600
The back of a TV looked like a
telephone switchboard.

28
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:25,480
Seven electronics companies
were tired of it.

29
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:26,200
Hitachi.

30
00:01:26,460 --> 00:01:27,060
Panasonic.

31
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:27,920
Philips.

32
00:01:28,420 --> 00:01:29,020
Silicon Image.

33
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:29,750
Sony.

34
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:30,370
Thomson.

35
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:31,000
Toshiba.

36
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:34,200
They sold rival TVs, and they
still sat down together.

37
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:37,506
In April 2002, they started
building one plug that

38
00:01:37,526 --> 00:01:40,100
carried picture and sound in
a single cable.

39
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:43,320
It even worked with the old
computer plug, called DVI.

40
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:47,000
Eight months later, on the ninth of
December, it was done.

41
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:49,120
They called it HDMI.

42
00:01:49,940 --> 00:01:52,060
But a plug needed one more thing.

43
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:53,060
Films.

44
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:55,234
Movies had gone digital,

45
00:01:55,254 --> 00:01:59,020
and a digital picture can be copied
perfectly, again and again.

46
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:02,260
The plan was to win over the film studios.

47
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:05,600
So the plug carried a lock,
supplied by Intel.

48
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:10,900
Fox, Universal, Warner Bros. and
Disney all backed it.

49
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:13,900
So did the big satellite and
cable companies.

50
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:16,180
Here's how the lock works.

51
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:18,200
Think of a badge.

52
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:22,800
Every device that has the lock
carries a set of secret keys.

53
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:27,460
When your streaming box meets your TV,
they check each other's badges.

54
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:31,920
If the TV has no badge, the box
won't send the film.

55
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:33,800
That is the whole trick.

56
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:36,900
Not a fence around the plug, but
a bouncer at the door.

57
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:38,780
And the badges cost money.

58
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:42,500
Making a device with the lock means
paying a fee every year,

59
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:43,833
fifteen thousand dollars,

60
00:02:43,853 --> 00:02:47,640
plus a price for each set of keys, which
gets cheaper the more you buy.

61
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:49,780
Can a lock like that be picked?

62
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:50,880
Yes.

63
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:51,840
And it was.

64
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:55,740
In September 2010, a master
key turned up online.

65
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:59,120
With it, anyone could make
keys that looked real.

66
00:03:00,020 --> 00:03:04,040
Intel said it seemed to have been worked
out from scratch, not leaked.

67
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:05,344
Either way,

68
00:03:05,364 --> 00:03:07,838
it warned it would take
legal action against

69
00:03:07,858 --> 00:03:10,580
anyone who built devices to
get around the lock.

70
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:16,340
Five years on, a small box called the HDFury
could strip the lock off a picture.

71
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:20,110
Warner Bros. sued on the last day of 2015.

72
00:03:20,420 --> 00:03:24,420
The box's maker argued it only lowered the
quality, which the law allows.

73
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:26,500
They settled five months later.

74
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:28,200
The lock got picked.

75
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:29,820
The chain didn't break.

76
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:31,000
Why not?

77
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:33,610
Because by then, the chain
had already moved.

78
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:37,120
Here is the whole loop, in four steps.

79
00:03:37,580 --> 00:03:40,800
One: studios and cable
companies back the plug.

80
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:44,070
Two: so the boxes people buy all use it.

81
00:03:44,100 --> 00:03:46,730
Three: so every TV has to have it.

82
00:03:46,760 --> 00:03:50,520
Four: so every TV maker signs
the contract, and pays.

83
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:53,260
More than two thousand one
hundred companies have.

84
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:55,040
Nobody voted for that.

85
00:03:55,360 --> 00:03:58,300
No law says every TV needs this plug.

86
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:01,390
The market simply arrived at a
place with one door.

87
00:04:01,420 --> 00:04:04,160
Try building a TV without it.

88
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:05,040
It would work.

89
00:04:05,140 --> 00:04:07,580
It just wouldn't plug into
most of what you own.

90
00:04:08,420 --> 00:04:10,720
Picture a club with one
entrance and a bouncer.

91
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:13,640
Anyone can build a nicer door
around the back.

92
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:17,980
But the party is inside, and the
party has the bouncer's badge.

93
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:19,840
So what does the club charge?

94
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:21,920
The contract is short on glamour.

95
00:04:22,140 --> 00:04:25,840
Every maker pays a yearly fee, ten
thousand dollars for a big one.

96
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:28,800
Small ones pay five thousand,
plus a dollar a unit.

97
00:04:29,160 --> 00:04:33,040
On top comes the fee for each device, and
this is the number in the title.

98
00:04:33,460 --> 00:04:36,300
As widely reported, it starts
at fifteen cents.

99
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:41,360
Put the HDMI logo on your product and
the fee drops to five cents.

100
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:44,330
Add the lock as well and it drops to four.

101
00:04:44,620 --> 00:04:45,560
Read that again.

102
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:48,760
The toll gets cheaper if you
advertise the toll company.

103
00:04:49,340 --> 00:04:54,420
The group's own logo rules say so, in
writing: use the logo, get a discount.

104
00:04:54,750 --> 00:04:55,470
Four cents.

105
00:04:55,800 --> 00:04:56,940
Less than a gumball.

106
00:04:57,360 --> 00:04:58,880
But that's the price for the giants.

107
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:00,720
Let's run it for a small maker.

108
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:05,380
Say you build ten thousand TVs a year, in a
garage that's turned into a factory.

109
00:05:05,880 --> 00:05:08,520
Your yearly fee is five thousand dollars.

110
00:05:08,760 --> 00:05:12,560
Then you pay a dollar on every TV,
just to be in the system.

111
00:05:13,180 --> 00:05:17,610
Then the per-device fee, at five cents,
comes to five hundred dollars.

112
00:05:17,800 --> 00:05:21,290
All told, about fifteen thousand
five hundred dollars.

113
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:24,180
That's a dollar fifty-five on every TV.

114
00:05:24,620 --> 00:05:27,620
Now run it for a giant
selling ten million TVs.

115
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:33,260
The yearly fee is ten thousand dollars, and the
per-device fee is four cents each.

116
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:35,940
It comes to about four cents a TV.

117
00:05:36,260 --> 00:05:37,680
So it isn't one price.

118
00:05:38,300 --> 00:05:41,780
The small maker pays nearly forty
times what the giant pays.

119
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:44,360
That's our own math on the reported fees.

120
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:48,000
A cheap contract weighs the most on
the people with the least.

121
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:49,570
And it's per device.

122
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:53,540
A TV with five ports on the back
pays once, not five times.

123
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:55,480
The fee comes with something else.

124
00:05:56,020 --> 00:05:58,240
A licence to the patents behind the plug.

125
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:00,660
That's the part most people never notice.

126
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:03,380
The contract doesn't just let
you use the name.

127
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:06,020
It lets you build the thing
without being sued.

128
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:09,180
But only for a real HDMI product.

129
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:10,320
Not a copy.

130
00:06:10,540 --> 00:06:11,460
Not a look-alike.

131
00:06:11,660 --> 00:06:14,280
The licence stops where the product stops.

132
00:06:14,940 --> 00:06:17,810
So how much does a few cents add up to?

133
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:22,640
In 2017 alone, the plug shipped in nearly
nine hundred million devices.

134
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:27,700
At four to fifteen cents each, that's tens of
millions of dollars, in a single year.

135
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:29,120
See how fast it grew.

136
00:06:29,270 --> 00:06:32,530
Six hundred million devices by early 2009.

137
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:34,790
Two billion by late 2011.

138
00:06:34,980 --> 00:06:37,330
Nearly ten billion by 2021.

139
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:39,460
Today, close to fourteen billion.

140
00:06:39,620 --> 00:06:41,800
That's about two for every
person on Earth.

141
00:06:42,340 --> 00:06:43,223
Multiply it out,

142
00:06:43,243 --> 00:06:47,119
and our own rough math says somewhere
between six hundred million and

143
00:06:47,139 --> 00:06:49,680
two billion dollars, over
twenty-three years.

144
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:51,820
Most likely near the bottom of that.

145
00:06:52,120 --> 00:06:53,060
That's real money.

146
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:55,200
But it isn't Big Tech money.

147
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:56,640
It doesn't need to be.

148
00:06:56,840 --> 00:06:58,880
Because the money was never the point.

149
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:02,120
The point is that nobody could say no.

150
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:03,580
Some tried.

151
00:07:04,020 --> 00:07:09,420
In 2006, a rival plug called DisplayPort
arrived, built for computer screens.

152
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:12,440
It came from a standards group,
and it was sold as free.

153
00:07:12,880 --> 00:07:14,540
It never took over the living room.

154
00:07:14,900 --> 00:07:16,480
The TVs were already taken.

155
00:07:17,020 --> 00:07:18,740
And it isn't quite free.

156
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:22,580
A group of patent owners asks
twenty cents a product.

157
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:26,060
That's more than the lowest
fee on the HDMI side.

158
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:30,120
Some of the names on that list are the
same ones that built HDMI.

159
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:32,860
So the escape route has a toll too.

160
00:07:33,340 --> 00:07:35,000
The group guards its name, too.

161
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:37,830
It has taken makers to court
for stamping the

162
00:07:37,850 --> 00:07:40,420
HDMI mark on products they
hadn't signed for.

163
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:43,534
Some were accused of doing it at a
big electronics show,

164
00:07:43,554 --> 00:07:44,980
right in front of the buyers.

165
00:07:45,800 --> 00:07:51,040
Then in 2011, the founders set up a
second body, called the HDMI Forum.

166
00:07:51,620 --> 00:07:53,700
It writes the new versions of the plug.

167
00:07:54,360 --> 00:07:57,560
HDMI Licensing Administrator
writes the contract.

168
00:07:57,940 --> 00:07:59,540
The Forum writes the rules.

169
00:08:00,400 --> 00:08:03,506
In June 2025, it released version 2.2,

170
00:08:03,526 --> 00:08:07,640
and the same licensing company
sells that one too.

171
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:10,120
Both trace back to the same founders.

172
00:08:11,120 --> 00:08:12,740
The names on the door change.

173
00:08:13,340 --> 00:08:15,520
Thomson is called Vantiva now.

174
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:17,820
The group doesn't change.

175
00:08:18,220 --> 00:08:20,670
Which brings us to a chip
company called Availink.

176
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:24,960
Availink made the little chips
that go inside TVs.

177
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:28,140
It sold them to other companies,
who built the sets.

178
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:32,060
So who owed the fee, the chip
maker or the TV maker?

179
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:34,060
The contract said the chip maker.

180
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:37,820
Availink signed it, and it was
licensed for about three years.

181
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:43,360
Then the deal ended, and the group behind HDMI
took it to court over unpaid fees.

182
00:08:43,820 --> 00:08:45,000
Availink hit back.

183
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:48,113
It argued that the group was
breaking competition law,

184
00:08:48,133 --> 00:08:50,140
and that the contract kept rivals out.

185
00:08:50,720 --> 00:08:54,878
On the last day of 2025, a federal
judge ruled for the group,

186
00:08:54,898 --> 00:08:57,980
and threw out every one of
Availink's claims.

187
00:08:58,360 --> 00:09:00,150
Call it a monopoly or don't.

188
00:09:00,180 --> 00:09:03,080
The judge found the contract had
not hurt competition.

189
00:09:03,680 --> 00:09:07,900
And the contract really did require the chip
company to pay fees on its chips.

190
00:09:08,340 --> 00:09:13,060
A week before the trial, Availink agreed
to pay fourteen million dollars.

191
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:15,500
Then it signed the contract again.

192
00:09:16,020 --> 00:09:17,320
Think about what that means.

193
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:20,664
A company whose whole
business was the chips

194
00:09:20,684 --> 00:09:23,323
inside TVs couldn't beat a contract that

195
00:09:23,343 --> 00:09:24,140
costs cents.

196
00:09:24,660 --> 00:09:26,720
The cheap fee was never the wall.

197
00:09:27,060 --> 00:09:30,185
The wall was everything
around it: the patents,

198
00:09:30,205 --> 00:09:34,020
the name, the lock, and a court
that upheld the contract.

199
00:09:34,400 --> 00:09:35,360
Let's be fair.

200
00:09:35,760 --> 00:09:37,520
There's a real case for all this.

201
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:42,000
One plug is why your console works
with any TV, in any country.

202
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:46,260
That takes testing, and it takes someone
keeping fakes off the shelf.

203
00:09:46,720 --> 00:09:48,520
That's the best argument for it.

204
00:09:48,720 --> 00:09:52,100
And when a plug does its job, nobody
thinks about it at all.

205
00:09:52,380 --> 00:09:56,080
And a judge looked at the
contract and found no harm.

206
00:09:56,700 --> 00:09:57,520
But hold on.

207
00:09:57,900 --> 00:09:59,540
The contract is about money.

208
00:09:59,840 --> 00:10:02,660
The Forum is about who gets
to see the plans.

209
00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:08,360
In 2021, the Forum closed the newest version of
the plug's rulebook to the public.

210
00:10:09,160 --> 00:10:12,430
Then AMD, one of the biggest
makers of graphics chips,

211
00:10:12,450 --> 00:10:15,680
tried to build an open-source
driver for it on Linux.

212
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:18,540
That's free code that anyone can read.

213
00:10:18,960 --> 00:10:20,230
They worked on it for months.

214
00:10:20,260 --> 00:10:23,367
In early 2024, an AMD engineer wrote:

215
00:10:23,387 --> 00:10:28,120
"The HDMI Forum has rejected our
proposal unfortunately.

216
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:32,699
At this time an open source HDMI
2.1 implementation is not

217
00:10:32,719 --> 00:10:37,020
possible without running afoul of the
HDMI Forum requirements."

218
00:10:37,620 --> 00:10:43,620
So on Linux, with an AMD card, the best
picture over HDMI is off the table.

219
00:10:44,020 --> 00:10:47,100
No four-K at a hundred and
twenty frames a second.

220
00:10:47,840 --> 00:10:49,460
AMD's advice to gamers?

221
00:10:49,800 --> 00:10:50,880
Use DisplayPort.

222
00:10:51,540 --> 00:10:53,160
And here's a small joke on top.

223
00:10:53,580 --> 00:11:01,760
Since 2023, a port can be sold as "HDMI 2.1"
and only do what the old one did.

224
00:11:02,300 --> 00:11:06,800
The group's answer, as reported: the
old version no longer exists.

225
00:11:07,220 --> 00:11:10,300
The only way to tell is the small
print on the spec sheet.

226
00:11:10,580 --> 00:11:11,920
Go back to that TV.

227
00:11:12,900 --> 00:11:15,260
The plug with two clipped corners.

228
00:11:15,860 --> 00:11:18,660
It looked like a piece of the
world, like a wall socket.

229
00:11:19,240 --> 00:11:20,840
It was a piece of a contract.

230
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:24,000
Nobody ever voted on the
plug behind your TV.

231
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:26,340
Nobody has ever seen the bill.

232
00:11:26,920 --> 00:11:28,920
The question isn't what it costs.

233
00:11:29,400 --> 00:11:33,160
It's who else has a lock on something you
never noticed you were using.
